INT. FUNERAL HOME KITCHEN - AFTERNOON

Linoleum. A casserole dish in every available space. A wall clock stopped at ten past four.

Six people share a room built for three.

GRACELAND "GRACIE" OSEI-LINDQVIST. 40s. Eldest daughter. Hands in the sink.

REUBEN OSEI-LINDQVIST. 40s. Her brother. Leaning on the fridge.

AUNT BERNADETTE VASQUEZ. 70s. Carries a tin of shortbread like a weapon.

HECTOR PRZYBYLSKI. 30s. Gracie's husband. Holds a dish towel he never uses.

LILIANA OSEI. 16. Gracie's daughter. Hood up.

THEO FINCH. 50s. Funeral director. Waiting.

Gracie scrubs a dish that is already clean.

THEO
Everything's arranged for three o'clock tomorrow.

Nobody answers.

THEO (CONT'D)
If the family would like a few more minutes...

AUNT BERNADETTE
We've had four days.

She sets the tin on the table. Opens it. Offers it to no one.

REUBEN
Is there coffee?

HECTOR
I'll make coffee.

He puts down the towel. Picks it up again. Opens a cupboard. Closes it.

GRACIE
Third shelf.

HECTOR
Right.

He finds the can. Fills the pot. Liliana watches him.

LILIANA
(to herself)
He's doing it wrong.

HECTOR
What?

LILIANA
Nothing.

REUBEN
(to the room)
Does anyone know where Dad kept the good suit?

Silence. Bernadette studies the shortbread. Hector studies the coffee can. Gracie turns off the tap.

GRACIE
Reuben.

REUBEN
It's a simple question.

GRACIE
It's in the hall closet.

REUBEN
Thank you.

GRACIE
Navy. Pressed. I did it Tuesday.

REUBEN
You didn't have to.

GRACIE
Someone did.

REUBEN
I would've.

GRACIE
(quiet)
Would you?

The kettle ticks on the stove. Theo clears his throat, but does not speak.

AUNT BERNADETTE
Children.

GRACIE
I'm not fighting.

^REUBEN
I'm not fighting.

Hector pours coffee into six mugs. Passes them around. Everyone takes one. No one drinks.

LILIANA
Mom. Can I go?

GRACIE
Where?

LILIANA
Out.

GRACIE
It's dark.

LILIANA
It's four-ten.

GRACIE
The clock is wrong.

LILIANA
Everything in here is wrong.

She stands. Hector reaches for her sleeve. She shrugs him off.

HECTOR
Lil.

LILIANA
Don't.

She goes. The back door bangs. Gracie wipes her hands on her apron very slowly, one finger at a time.

THEO
(gently)
Mrs. Przybylski...

GRACIE
It's Osei-Lindqvist. Still.

THEO
Of course. Forgive me.

GRACIE
(to the room)
Does anyone want to see him?

The question lands like a dropped plate. Reuben stares into his coffee. Bernadette lowers the tin lid. Hector sets his mug down.

AUNT BERNADETTE
I do.

GRACIE
Auntie.

AUNT BERNADETTE
I've seen him in every state a man can be in. I'll see this one, too.

She stands. Straightens her cardigan.

AUNT BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
Who is coming with me?

Nobody moves. Then Reuben puts down his mug. Then Gracie. Then, last, Hector. Theo opens the kitchen door and stands aside.

They file out into the hall. The coffee cools. The clock stays where it is, ten past four.
